        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can grass seed grow if you just throw it on the ground?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some will, but results are usually poor. Seed lying on hard soil or thatch dries out quickly, washes away, and is eaten by birds. Loosening the soil surface, raking seed in lightly so it is barely covered, and keeping it moist dramatically improves germination. Seeding right after core aeration is the easiest way to get good soil contact on an existing lawn."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I water grass seed every day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, during germination. Most new seed needs light watering one to three times a day to keep the surface moist, depending on heat, wind and sun. Once seedlings are about an inch tall, water less often but longer. After the first few mows, move to deep watering once or twice a week, about an inch in total including rainfall."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it too late to plant grass seed in October?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For cool-season grass in the northern US, early October is often late but still workable in milder areas, as long as you have about six weeks before the first hard frost. Later than that, consider dormant seeding in late fall instead, where seed sits until spring. For warm-season grass, October is too late; wait for late spring."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will grass seed germinate on top of the soil?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can germinate on the surface if kept constantly moist, but it is much less reliable. Seed on top of the soil dries quickly between waterings, roots struggle to anchor, and birds eat it. A light covering of about an eighth to a quarter inch of soil, or a thin straw mulch, gives far better and more even results."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know if my grass seed is germinating?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Look closely at ground level. New seedlings are thin, thread-like shoots, often pale green, and are easy to miss when standing. You can also check a few seeds; germinating ones will have split and show a tiny white root or shoot. Fast species show within a week or two, while bluegrass may take up to a month."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you walk on grass seed after planting?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Keep foot traffic to a minimum until the new grass has been mowed several times. Walking on seed pushes it too deep or compacts the soil, and walking on young seedlings crushes and uproots them. If you must cross the area to move hoses, use the same path or lay down boards to spread out your weight."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does grass seed go bad?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, slowly. Seed stored cool and dry can keep reasonable germination for two to three years, but viability falls over time, especially in heat or humidity. Check the test date and germination percentage on the label. If you have old seed, sprout a sample between damp paper towels for a week or two to see what share still germinates."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
