    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most grass seed germinates in 5 to 30 days: perennial ryegrass in about 5 to 10 days, tall fescue in 7 to 14, Bermuda in roughly 10 to 30, and Kentucky bluegrass in 14 to 30, assuming the soil is warm enough and stays consistently moist. From there, expect the first mow in about three to eight weeks and a lawn that can handle normal use in two to three months. Seed sown into cold or drying soil can sit for weeks without sprouting, so the real answer depends as much on when and how you seed as on the species in the bag.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Weeks to Sprout, Months to a Lawn
          </h2>

          <p>
            Growing grass from seed happens in stages, and people often mean different things when they ask how long it takes. Germination is when the seed splits and a tiny shoot appears, which is the number on most seed bags. Then the seedlings grow to mowing height, which takes several more weeks. Finally the young grass develops enough roots and density to handle foot traffic, heat and drought, which takes a couple of months and, for some species, a full growing season.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Timeline</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-30 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Germination, by species</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-8 weeks</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">First mow</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-12 weeks</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Light regular use</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 season</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Full maturity</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The fastest grasses are ryegrasses, which is why they are in so many quick-fix mixes and are used to overseed dormant Bermuda for winter color. The slowest common lawn grasses are Kentucky bluegrass among cool-season types and bahia, centipede and zoysia among warm-season types. Mixes often contain both fast and slow species, so you will see the ryegrass come up first and the bluegrass fill in over the following weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those ranges assume good conditions: the right soil temperature for the species, seed in firm contact with soil, and steady moisture. Change any of those and the clock slows or stops. Our <a href="/best-grass-seed/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best grass seed picks</a> cover which species and blends suit sun, shade and climate, but even the best seed follows the same rules on timing and water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-species" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Long Does Each Grass Type Take to Germinate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            These ranges reflect the figures commonly published by seed producers and university extension services under good conditions. Use the lower end for warm soil in the ideal window and steady moisture, and the upper end for cooler soil, early or late seeding, or less consistent watering.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grass</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Germination (days)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Perennial ryegrass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cool-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fastest common lawn grass</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Annual ryegrass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cool-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Temporary cover; dies out in a year</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tall fescue</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cool-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-14</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most popular all-around choice</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fine fescues</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cool-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-14</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good shade tolerance</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kentucky bluegrass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cool-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14-30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow start, spreads to fill gaps</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bermuda</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Warm-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hulled seed is faster than unhulled</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Zoysia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Warm-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14-21+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow; often planted as sod or plugs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Centipede</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Warm-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14-28</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow and low-input</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bahia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Warm-season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21-28+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow to establish from seed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Kentucky bluegrass is the one that tests patience most. It often shows little or nothing for two weeks, and homeowners give up and reseed just before it emerges. It also establishes slowly afterward, though it spreads by underground rhizomes and eventually fills gaps on its own. That is why bluegrass is so often blended with perennial ryegrass, which provides quick cover while the bluegrass catches up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Among warm-season grasses, Bermuda is the most practical to seed, especially hulled seed, which has the outer husk removed and germinates faster than unhulled seed. Zoysia, centipede and bahia are slower and more often planted as sod or plugs. St. Augustine is almost always installed as sod or plugs because seed is not commonly available for typical lawn varieties.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seed coatings and mixes change what you see, not the biology. Some seed is coated with a water-holding material, which can help in tricky conditions but also means you are paying partly for coating by weight. A sun and shade mix with three or four species will sprout in waves. If you are judging progress, look for the slower species' fine shoots among the faster grass before deciding a spot has failed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="soil-temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Does Soil Temperature Matter More Than the Calendar?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Seed germinates in response to soil temperature and moisture, not the date. Cool-season grasses germinate best when soil is roughly 50 to 65 degrees Fahrenheit, which typically comes with daytime air temperatures of about 60 to 75. Warm-season grasses need soil of at least 65 to 70 degrees, which usually means air temperatures in the 80s and nights that stay warm.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cool-season seed in early fall, warm-season seed in late spring</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Early fall is the best time to seed cool-season grasses: warm soil, cooler air, fewer weeds and a long growing period before winter. Late spring to early summer is the best time to seed warm-season grasses, once soil is reliably above 65 degrees.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For cool-season lawns, early fall beats spring because the soil is still warm from summer while the air cools, which speeds germination and root growth. Weeds such as crabgrass are winding down. In most of the north, the window runs from roughly mid-August to mid-September, and the usual rule is to seed at least 45 days before your first expected frost so seedlings are established before winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spring seeding of cool-season grass works but is harder. The soil is cold early on, so germination is slow, crabgrass competes as soon as soil warms, and summer heat arrives before seedlings have deep roots. Seed sown into cold soil in early spring can sit for weeks waiting, during which time birds eat it, rain washes it, and it may rot. Spring-seeded lawns often need extra watering through their first summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warm-season seed sown too early simply waits, and much of it is lost. Bermuda seeded into 55-degree soil can take a month or longer and come up thin. Waiting until late spring or early summer means much faster germination and a long, hot growing season for the seedlings to spread. Dormant seeding, sowing cool-season seed in late fall or winter so it germinates in spring, is a separate technique and naturally takes months rather than days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="watering" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Should You Water New Grass Seed?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Consistent moisture is the single most important factor once the seed is down. Seed that absorbs water and starts germinating, then dries out, usually dies. The goal for the first few weeks is to keep the top half inch to inch of soil constantly moist, not soaked. In practice that means light watering one to three times a day, depending on heat, wind and sun, for roughly five to ten minutes each time with a typical sprinkler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a gentle spray. A strong stream washes seed into piles and low spots, leaving bare patches and clumps. A sprinkler or a nozzle on a mist or shower setting works best; our <a href="/best-garden-hose-nozzle/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garden hose nozzle picks</a> include models with fine settings that suit new seed. Watch for puddles and runoff, and cut back if you see either.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How often</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Goal</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Seeding to germination</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 times a day, lightly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Top half inch to inch always moist</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Germination to about 1 inch tall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Once or twice a day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keep surface moist, start wetting deeper</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1 inch to first mow</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 1-2 days, longer sessions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moisture deeper into root zone</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">After first few mows</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 times a week</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 inch of water per week total</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            As seedlings grow, shift gradually from frequent light watering to less frequent, deeper watering. This encourages roots to grow down instead of staying near the surface. By the time you have mowed a few times, you should be on a normal schedule of deep watering once or twice a week, around an inch total including rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            A thin layer of mulch helps a lot in hot or windy weather. Weed-free straw spread thinly, so you can still see about half the soil through it, holds moisture and protects seed from birds and washing. Avoid hay, which carries weed seeds, and avoid thick layers that smother seedlings. Peat moss or a thin topdressing of compost works for small patches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-mow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Can You Mow New Grass for the First Time?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mow when the new grass is about a third taller than the height you plan to keep it. For most cool-season lawns kept at around three inches, that means mowing when seedlings reach about four inches. For Bermuda kept short, it means mowing much sooner. Following the usual one-third rule protects young plants from losing too much leaf at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing varies by species. Perennial ryegrass often reaches mowing height in three to four weeks. Tall fescue usually takes four to five weeks. Kentucky bluegrass can take six to eight weeks or more. In mixes, the faster grasses reach height first, so you may need to mow while the slower species are still small; set the mower high and they will be fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a sharp blade. Young grass is tender and shallow-rooted, and a dull blade tears and even pulls seedlings out of the soil. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to sharpen mower blades</a> covers how to tell when yours needs it. Mow when the grass and soil are dry, so wheels do not rut the soft surface, and avoid sharp turns that scuff seedlings.
          </p>

          <p>
            A lighter push mower is gentler on new lawns than a heavy riding mower for the first few cuts. Mulching or bagging clippings are both fine; mulched clippings are small and break down quickly. Keep mowing regularly once you start, since letting new grass get very tall and then cutting it short stresses the seedlings more than frequent, light mowing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="not-growing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Is My Grass Seed Not Growing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before deciding seed has failed, check the calendar against the species. Two weeks without anything visible is normal for Kentucky bluegrass and many warm-season grasses. Get down at ground level and look closely; new grass seedlings are thin as threads and easy to miss from standing height. If the expected window has passed, work through the common causes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seed buried too deep is a frequent problem. Grass seed should be covered by only about an eighth to a quarter of an inch of soil. Seed buried an inch deep may never emerge. The opposite problem, seed lying on hard, untilled ground or on top of thatch, means poor soil contact; it dries out between waterings and roots cannot get into the soil. Raking lightly after spreading, or seeding after aeration, fixes both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drying out is the other big cause. Seed that started to germinate and then dried for even a day can die. Cold soil, as covered above, can stall germination entirely. A crabgrass pre-emergent applied in spring will stop most grass seed from germinating too, and its effect can last for weeks to months depending on the product, so check what has been applied to the lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seed quality matters as well. Seed labels list a germination percentage and a test date. Old seed loses viability, especially if stored somewhere hot or damp, and cheap seed can carry a high share of filler, weed seed or slow, coarse varieties. Birds and washouts from heavy rain account for the rest; a light straw mulch and gentle watering help with both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thick thatch is a hidden barrier when overseeding an existing lawn. Seed caught in a spongy thatch layer cannot reach soil. If you measure more than half an inch of thatch, our guide on <a href="/when-to-dethatch-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to dethatch your lawn</a> explains how to deal with it before seeding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="establishment" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Long Until a New Lawn Is Fully Established?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most new lawns can take light, occasional use after about eight to twelve weeks, once they have been mowed several times and roots have grown down into the soil. Heavy use, such as kids playing ball or dogs running the same path, should wait longer, ideally until after a full growing season. Kentucky bluegrass in particular keeps thickening for months as its rhizomes spread.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feeding helps establishment. A starter fertilizer at seeding gives seedlings early nutrients, and a second, light feeding about six to eight weeks later supports root and shoot growth. Our guide on <a href="/when-to-fertilize-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to fertilize your lawn</a> covers how to fold that into the normal schedule. Hold off on weed control products until the new grass has been mowed three or four times, unless the label specifically says it is safe on new seedlings.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cool-season lawns seeded in early fall, the timeline works out neatly: germination in September, first mows in October, and a lawn that goes into winter well rooted and comes back thick in spring. Remove fallen leaves gently from new grass, using a light rake or blower rather than heavy raking, so seedlings are not smothered or pulled out.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are overseeding an existing lawn rather than starting from bare soil, aeration before seeding greatly improves the result, because seed falls into the holes where moisture and soil contact are best. Our guide on <a href="/when-to-aerate-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to aerate your lawn</a> covers how to time the two together. A <a href="/best-broadcast-spreader/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">broadcast spreader</a> puts seed down far more evenly than spreading by hand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Patience is the hardest part. Most failed seedings come from stopping watering too soon, seeding at the wrong time of year, or giving up on slow species before they emerge. Seed in the right season for your grass, keep the soil moist until the grass is established, mow high with a sharp blade, and the lawn will usually fill in faster than you expect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Burying the seed too deep</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grass seed needs light and close soil contact, not a deep covering. Aim for an eighth to a quarter of an inch of soil over the seed, which a light raking usually provides. Seed buried an inch deep often never emerges, and the lawn comes up thin no matter how carefully you water it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the surface dry out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Once seed absorbs water and starts germinating, a single dry afternoon can kill it. Keep the top half inch to inch of soil moist with light watering one to three times a day until the grass is up and growing, then shift gradually to deeper, less frequent watering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Seeding into cold soil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cool-season seed sown into early spring soil and warm-season seed sown before soil reaches about 65 degrees can sit for weeks. Birds eat it, rain washes it and some rots. Seed cool-season grass in early fall and warm-season grass in late spring for much faster germination.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying crabgrass preventer at seeding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most crabgrass pre-emergents stop grass seed from sprouting as well as weeds, often for several weeks or months. Do not apply one before or right after seeding unless the label specifically says it is safe for new seed. Otherwise the seed you paid for will never germinate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing too late or too low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Letting new grass grow very tall and then cutting it short stresses seedlings and can pull them out. Mow once the grass is about a third taller than your target height, use a sharp blade, mow when the soil is dry, and never remove more than a third at once.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can grass seed grow if you just throw it on the ground?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some will, but results are usually poor. Seed lying on hard soil or thatch dries out quickly, washes away, and is eaten by birds. Loosening the soil surface, raking seed in lightly so it is barely covered, and keeping it moist dramatically improves germination. Seeding right after core aeration is the easiest way to get good soil contact on an existing lawn.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I water grass seed every day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, during germination. Most new seed needs light watering one to three times a day to keep the surface moist, depending on heat, wind and sun. Once seedlings are about an inch tall, water less often but longer. After the first few mows, move to deep watering once or twice a week, about an inch in total including rainfall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it too late to plant grass seed in October?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cool-season grass in the northern US, early October is often late but still workable in milder areas, as long as you have about six weeks before the first hard frost. Later than that, consider dormant seeding in late fall instead, where seed sits until spring. For warm-season grass, October is too late; wait for late spring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will grass seed germinate on top of the soil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can germinate on the surface if kept constantly moist, but it is much less reliable. Seed on top of the soil dries quickly between waterings, roots struggle to anchor, and birds eat it. A light covering of about an eighth to a quarter inch of soil, or a thin straw mulch, gives far better and more even results.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my grass seed is germinating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look closely at ground level. New seedlings are thin, thread-like shoots, often pale green, and are easy to miss when standing. You can also check a few seeds; germinating ones will have split and show a tiny white root or shoot. Fast species show within a week or two, while bluegrass may take up to a month.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you walk on grass seed after planting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep foot traffic to a minimum until the new grass has been mowed several times. Walking on seed pushes it too deep or compacts the soil, and walking on young seedlings crushes and uproots them. If you must cross the area to move hoses, use the same path or lay down boards to spread out your weight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does grass seed go bad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, slowly. Seed stored cool and dry can keep reasonable germination for two to three years, but viability falls over time, especially in heat or humidity. Check the test date and germination percentage on the label. If you have old seed, sprout a sample between damp paper towels for a week or two to see what share still germinates.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

