        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Grass seed usually sprouts in one to four weeks depending on species: ryegrass fastest, then fescues, with Kentucky bluegrass and most warm-season grasses taking longest. Expect the first mow three to eight weeks after seeding and a lawn ready for regular use in two to three months, with full maturity after a growing season.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The biggest factors are in your control. Seed in the right season for your grass type, get the seed into light contact with loose soil, keep the surface moist until it is up, and mow high with a sharp blade. Most failed seedings come from cold soil, dry soil or impatience, not from bad seed.
          </p>
        </div>

